        <h2>Koggala Lake</h2>
        <p>A kilometre east of Ahangama, Koggala Lake is one of Sri Lanka's largest natural lakes — a broad, calm body of water dotted with small islands, some inhabited, others given over to cinnamon plantations and Buddhist temples. Boat trips out to the lake's islands are available from the lakeside road for LKR 1,500–2,500 per boat; the most visited is the island temple, Kataluwa Purvarama, though the cinnamon-island stop — where a small cooperative demonstrates the hand-peeling of Ceylon cinnamon — is the most interesting.</p>
        <p>Koggala Lake is also exceptional for birdwatching. Purple herons, painted storks, cormorants and kingfishers are resident year-round; the lake's reed beds attract migrants in season. Early morning boat trips offer the best wildlife sightings.</p>
        <p>On the lakeside, the <strong>Martin Wickramasinghe Folk Museum</strong> — named after one of Sri Lanka's most important 20th-century Sinhala novelists, who was born in Koggala — is a thoughtfully curated collection of traditional rural craft, rural life and literary heritage. One of the most charming small museums on the south coast, and free to enter.</p>

        <h2>Where to Eat</h2>
        <p>Ahangama punches well above its weight on food. The fish market near the junction sells the morning's catch direct from boats, and the surrounding small restaurants and family-run cadés turn this into some of the best fresh seafood on the south coast. Look for grilled reef fish, devilled cuttlefish and prawn curry at local restaurants where a full meal rarely costs more than LKR 1,200–1,800. Several more upmarket restaurants have opened along the coastal road as boutique hotel development has increased — these are good, if considerably pricier. The best budget option is simply to follow the fishing boats to the nearest beachside kadé.</p>

        <h2>Day Trips from Ahangama</h2>
        <p>Ahangama's central south coast position makes it an excellent base for day trips. <a href="/places/galle">Galle Fort</a> is 10km east — 15 minutes by tuk-tuk — and easily visited in a half-day. <a href="/places/unawatuna">Unawatuna</a> is 8km east, <a href="/places/weligama">Weligama</a> 5km west and <a href="/places/hikkaduwa">Hikkaduwa</a> 20km west. <a href="/places/mirissa">Mirissa</a>, for whale watching, is 15km east. The <a href="/things-to-do/rope-swing">Dalawella rope swing</a> is a 20-minute tuk-tuk ride towards Unawatuna. Brief Garden — the extraordinary tropical garden created by Bevis Bawa — is about 45 minutes by road near Beruwala, best visited on a dedicated half-day.</p>

        <h2>Getting to Ahangama</h2>
        <p>Ahangama has its own railway station on the Colombo–Matara coastal line — trains from Colombo Fort take approximately 3 hours; from Galle, it is one stop (15 minutes). The station is a 5-minute walk from the coast. By road, Ahangama sits on the A2 coastal highway: tuk-tuks from Galle cost around LKR 500–700; from Weligama, LKR 300–400. Colombo to Ahangama via the Southern Expressway takes under 2 hours by car. Intercity buses from Galle and Matara stop at Ahangama junction regularly throughout the day.</p>

